e extent that  those people might choose to go  about insurance so for about a quarter  of the people they'll be paying the full  cost of the increase in their Obamacare  premium that's right they're paying the  full cost of their plan already they're  getting no subsidies right and so when  the plan price goes up they will pay the  full cost of the increase as well and so  that's gonna change the calculation and  so that means if it's a 25% increase on  a $400 a month premium it would go up to  $500 that's right although it's  important to understand that these are  average yeses yeah and so in some places  the increases are gonna be somewhat  smaller but in some places we've seen  increases in the 30 40 even 60% range  and so it's really gonna vary by region  in particular rural regions are gonna  see the biggest increases and then in  2019 the subsidy caps go away so that  means that the the government will be  subsidizing less of the increases  explaining how that works right now the  situation is that if you are subsidized  most of the increase in your premiums  will be covered by the subsidy but  starting in a few years people even  people who get subsidies are going to  start to feel the pain of increases  because the way the subsidy is  calculated is gonna change and so what's  gonna happen is that this insulation  that people have this kind of buffer  from premium increases exists for right  now but is going to go away in a few  years and that is going to make it even  more likely that those people are might  choose to pay a penalty rather than to  purchase insurance and and I mean those  the premiums are going up because the  health insurance companies miscalculated  how much on average it was going to cost  to cover people the premiums are going  up because the insurance pool turned out  to be somewhat sicker than expected and  therefore more expensive and also it  turned out to be smaller than expected  so if you look at the initial  projections from the